<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulaimanu of Kano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1807">1807</a>–<a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sulaiman dan Abahama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulaimanu was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, ruling from 1807 to 1819. A scholar and a flagbearer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, he was chosen to lead Kano after the jihadists overthrew its last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-alwali-ii">Muhammad Alwali</a>. He governed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-dabo">Ibrahim Dabo</a>, founder of the dynasty that still rules Kano.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Muhammad Bello and brother Abdullahi dan Fodio led the campaigns against Gobir, while flagbearers such as <b>Sulaimanu</b> in Kano, Musa in Zaria and Modibbo Adama in Adamawa carved out new emirates; within some five years virtually every major Hausa ruler had been overthrown or subordinated, creating…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The scholar <b>Sulaimanu</b> became the first emir, and in 1819 he was succeeded by Ibrahim Dabo, whose descendants, the Dabo dynasty, have provided Kano's emirs ever since.</blockquote><a href="/state/kano-emirate">Kano Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
